speechtext:
I do not think that I strayed very far from the item, but perhaps it does not suit some hon. members to hear these things. The question of cream separators in the west is a vital one, and in the near future it may become of still greater importance to that part of the country, because if we are put out of business in one line we shall have to go into another, and the two central provinces, about which we hear so much, will have a chance to taste some of the conditions we in the west are up against. About two months ago the price of butter fat in the west was down to 16 cents a pound. I wonder what it was in Ontario at that time. I know it was not anywhere near that. The hon. member for Selkirk put up a very strong case for the free importation of cream separators!, and I have to support him in that plea. To some hon.-members who are supposed to be greatly interested in labour conditions, who make statements such as they made here today, I would suggest that:
A little learning is a dangerous thing;
Drink deep, or taste not the Pierian spring:
